### 4.7 Results from Clustering

The basic principle of the clustering algorithm adopted in the present analysis is as follows :

Figure 4.6: Distribution of energy deposited (\(E_{dep}\)) in an isolated cell in simulation.

* A super-cluster is first formed connecting all the cells having non-zero ADC or \(E_{dep}\) values.
* Cells are ordered in the descending order of their ADC value (data) or energy deposited (\(E_{dep}\)) (simulation) values.
* Super-clusters are broken into refined clusters.
* The final cluster output is written in terms of (a) cluster ADC or \(E_{dep}\), (b) cluster X, Y positions, (c) cluster \(\eta\) and \(\phi\) values, (d) number of cells in a cluster.

Fig. 4.8 shows typical features of the clusters from the PMD, for example (a) shows the distribution of the number of clusters without any threshold in the PMD. It is seen that the number of clusters are much less as compared to the number of hits (Fig. 4.7(a)). The correlation between the TPC reference multiplicity (charged particle multiplicity within \(|\eta|<0.5\)) and the number of clusters in the PMD is displayed in Fig. 4.8(b). Distribution of the number of cells in a cluster is plotted in Fig. 4.8(c) and the distribution of energy in a cluster i.e., \(E_{dep}\), is shown in Fig. 4.8(d). The physics performance of the preshower plane of the PMD depends on the two quantities :

* Photon counting efficiency (\(\epsilon_{\gamma}\)), and
* Purity (\(f_{p}\)) of the photon samples.

These two quantities are discussed in Section 4.9 and 4.10, respectively.

